WebVn TODO
Sequenced work is derived from the design docs in design-docs/ and is a snapshot
as of 2026-08-25 - the dependency edges hold until the work itself changes them,
the size guesses are worth less.

Landed: D (asset manifest), 2026-08-28. Both steps of .scratch/asset-manifest/:
a project declares its own assets in manifest.yaml, and the demo ships the first one.
Its two chains - storage, and asset/label completions in the editor - are now rooted
on what it left behind rather than on it.

Landed: G and the sprite half of .scratch/sprites/, 2026-08-28, as one format
change under formatVersion: 1. All three declarations are keyed maps the script names
ids from; audio carries title/artist and the pause menu shows a now-playing line;
`show` takes an id, so an actor can be on screen more than once. E (AssetResolver) is
unblocked - src/domRenderer/assetPaths.ts is the seam it slots into - and the manifest
shape is settled ahead of the storage chain, which was the point of doing it first.

Landed: .scratch/manifest-editor/, 2026-08-29. manifest.yaml is a second buffer in the
editor, adopted on blur; the ?vn= payload is a two-document stream carrying it; saves are
keyed vn-save-<id> from the manifest rather than the hardcoded "test"; and the asset
loaders report a declared file that is not there instead of hanging on it. That takes the
URL-payload item and its save-keying child below, and the ordering edge between them.
VnEditor has tests and a startEditor harness for the first time, which is the start of T.

Landed: the whole of .scratch/project-storage/ tranche 1, 2026-08-30. A project lives in
OPFS at projects/<id>/{manifest.yaml,script.yaml,assets/}, the editor boots out of it and
stores both buffers back on a 2s debounce with a stored/unstored/failed indicator, an asset's
bytes come through an AssetResolver (relative paths for the player, OPFS for the editor), and
a second tab on one project is refused by a navigator.locks lock rather than racing the first.
That takes E and its object-URL test below, and the whole "OPFS project store" node. What
hangs off it - the picker, rename, import, export, the nag - is tranche 2 and 3 and unchanged
below. One finding is filed in ROUGH_EDGES.md, and it turned out to predate this work: an
unanimated background render reports finished one frame before it paints, so the editor shows a
one-frame white flash on every script load. It was first blamed on OPFS object URLs and is not
theirs - a clone of a loaded image is ready synchronously either way.

Extracted: the storage chain's first six tickets, 2026-08-29, as .scratch/project-storage/,
then grilled over five rounds on 2026-08-30. E and its object-URL test are two of them; the
OPFS store below is split into three, because one "L" line was hiding a filesystem layer, a
project layer and a boot path. navigator.locks moved up from the picker's tranche to sit beside
storing - before storing exists a second tab costs nothing, and after it two debounced writers
share one copy of the author's work, so the two ship together. The picker and everything past
it stay unextracted - their shape depends on the store's API being real. That spec also lists
what the design doc predates and nobody should re-derive.

The grilling settled: the resolver resolves paths rather than ids and only loadAsset consults
it; assets live under assets/ and the code moves to match; a browser without OPFS gets no
editor rather than a memory-only one; storing is debounced with a stored/unstored/failed
indicator above the buffer's right corner; and "store" is now a reserved word - see CONTEXT.md,
because "save" is the player's.

Landed: the whole of .scratch/project-library/ - tranche 2 - 2026-09-05. An author has a library: the
picker is the front door, projects can be made, opened, deleted and renamed, a rename's directory
follows the manifest's id and is recoverable from any crash state, and the editor can put a project
down without leaving anything of it running. Ticket 06 followed on 2026-09-05: the open project is
in the URL as ?project=<directory>, so a reload - and webpack-dev-server's reload-on-save - lands
back in the project, and back and forward walk the views the author walked. What is left of
design-docs/PROJECT_STORAGE.md is import, export, the linked folder and the nag - tranche 3, which
needs its own spec. Each ticket's Comments record what changed under it during implementation, of
which there was a fair amount.

Extracted: tranche 2, 2026-09-05, as .scratch/project-library/ - six tickets covering the
three nodes below that are not import or export. Teardown first, because ProjectStoring's three
page listeners are a measured data-loss bug the moment two boots share a page; then the picker,
new/delete, rename with its crash recovery, and per-project size with persist(). The export nag
that sits with persist() below did not go with it and is now tranche 3's: there is nothing to
export yet, so a "last exported" date would be a field nothing writes.

Extracted: tranche 3, 2026-09-06, as .scratch/project-archive/ - zip export and import, three
tickets, after five rounds of grilling. It is deliberately smaller than the node below suggests:
URL import, the published static folder, single-file HTML, re-encoding and content addressing all
move to tranche 4, against this document's and the design doc's own ordering. The argument that put
URL import first was that it would retire seedDemoProject, and tranche 2 already retired the half of
that which mattered - so URL import now deletes scaffolding that works, while zip is what makes
"your project can leave the browser" true for the first time. Every destructive dialog in the app
currently says so out loud: "There is no export yet, so nothing outside this browser has a copy."

Settled: H, 2026-09-06, by measurement rather than by building anything. @zip.js/zip.js pinned to
lib/zip-core-custom.js is 30.6KB gzipped for reader and writer, spawns no workers at all, and
delegates deflate to the platform's Compression Streams; unzipit + client-zip is 6.7KB but cannot
pipe an entry into a FileSystemWritableFileStream. The doc's 79-85KB guess was stale both ways. The
sharp finding is that the package's *default* entry bakes the build machine's file:// path into the
bundle via import.meta.url and then loses its workers silently - see design-docs/PROJECT_STORAGE.md.

Sizes: S = hours, M = a day or two, L = several days.


